1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,193
♪ ♪

2
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,337
♪ ♪

3
00:00:49,483 --> 00:00:51,483
♪ ♪

4
00:01:04,864 --> 00:01:07,799
♪ ♪

5
00:01:30,424 --> 00:01:32,357
♪ ♪

6
00:01:55,849 --> 00:01:57,782
♪ ♪

7
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:18,236
♪ ♪

8
00:02:41,461 --> 00:02:43,695
Friends, we are gathered here

9
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:46,364
to pay our final respects

10
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:49,500
to our good friend Daniel
Bartholomew Kingsley.

11
00:02:50,804 --> 00:02:52,704
Now, our thoughts
and our prayers

12
00:02:52,739 --> 00:02:55,106
go to his loving wife Felicia

13
00:02:55,141 --> 00:02:57,676
and to his son Aaron,

14
00:02:57,711 --> 00:03:01,479
his daughter Ellen
and her husband Jim

15
00:03:01,515 --> 00:03:04,516
and his four beloved
grandchildren,

16
00:03:04,551 --> 00:03:08,152
and to his many, many friends.

17
00:03:08,188 --> 00:03:10,722
I had the good fortune
to be one of them,

18
00:03:10,757 --> 00:03:13,991
and how I look forward
to that glorious day

19
00:03:14,027 --> 00:03:17,563
when we shall be together
again on Jordan's far shore.

20
00:03:17,598 --> 00:03:21,132
But until then,
Daniel Bartholomew...

21
00:03:21,168 --> 00:03:22,734
D.B., as those of us

22
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:25,537
who were blessed with
his friendship knew him...

23
00:03:25,573 --> 00:03:29,074
Rest in peace, old friend.

24
00:03:29,109 --> 00:03:32,743
May the grace of God
be with you all. Amen.

25
00:03:32,779 --> 00:03:35,046
Amen.

26
00:03:38,552 --> 00:03:44,322
You know, that wasn't the reunion
we hoped it would be, was it?

27
00:03:44,358 --> 00:03:47,692
I know, but that's
the way life is.

28
00:03:47,727 --> 00:03:48,771
Yeah.

29
00:03:48,795 --> 00:03:51,763
Friends part, miss each other.

30
00:03:51,798 --> 00:03:54,210
Talk about getting
back together,

31
00:03:54,234 --> 00:03:56,613
and somehow, it never happens.

32
00:03:56,637 --> 00:03:58,570
And then, one
day, it's too late.

33
00:04:01,575 --> 00:04:03,909
Hey, how about this?

34
00:04:03,944 --> 00:04:07,012
How about all four of us have
dinner at Ray Templin's Bar?

35
00:04:07,047 --> 00:04:09,025
We'll drink a toast to old
D.B. What do you say?

36
00:04:09,049 --> 00:04:11,260
I haven't been there in so long.

37
00:04:11,284 --> 00:04:12,884
Think the reverend will join us?

38
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:14,730
You ever know
Jimmy to miss a party?

39
00:04:14,754 --> 00:04:16,065
Of course, that's the old days.

40
00:04:16,089 --> 00:04:17,522
Before he got religion.

41
00:04:17,558 --> 00:04:19,724
Well, there are four of us left.

42
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:21,193
Tell the truth, Ben.

43
00:04:21,228 --> 00:04:23,361
40 years ago, would
you have ever imagined

44
00:04:23,396 --> 00:04:24,996
that any of us would
have come this far?

45
00:04:25,032 --> 00:04:28,733
I don't know, but we got
lucky, I guess. Real lucky.

46
00:04:28,769 --> 00:04:31,703
The way I see it, we
made our own luck.

47
00:04:45,785 --> 00:04:47,519
Ben?

48
00:04:47,554 --> 00:04:49,554
Hi.

49
00:04:49,589 --> 00:04:50,722
Hi.

50
00:04:51,791 --> 00:04:53,669
Dad asked me to give you this.

51
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:55,127
That? What is it?

52
00:04:55,162 --> 00:04:56,595
I don't know.

53
00:04:56,630 --> 00:04:58,608
He gave it to me about
a week before he died.

54
00:04:58,632 --> 00:05:00,343
Said it was for your eyes only.

55
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:01,599
Oh.

56
00:05:01,635 --> 00:05:04,669
You really meant
a lot to him, Ben.

57
00:05:07,674 --> 00:05:10,342
This court is in session.

58
00:05:10,377 --> 00:05:13,612
Mr. Taft, are you ready to
question the prospective jurors?

59
00:05:13,647 --> 00:05:15,180
I am, Your Honor.

60
00:05:15,215 --> 00:05:16,448
Proceed.

61
00:05:16,483 --> 00:05:18,716
Mr. Morgan, what
is your occupation?

62
00:05:18,752 --> 00:05:21,719
I recently retired from
Hoffman Tool and Die.

63
00:05:21,755 --> 00:05:23,689
42 years, don't you know?

64
00:05:23,724 --> 00:05:27,092
When we first started, we
used to put in a 12-hour day.

65
00:05:28,762 --> 00:05:31,063
Now I don't know
what to do with my time.

66
00:05:31,098 --> 00:05:33,364
Mr. Morgan, in the case of...

67
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:35,200
<i>Dear Ben,</i>

68
00:05:35,235 --> 00:05:39,738
<i>I'm about to tell you
a 35-year-old secret.</i>

69
00:05:39,773 --> 00:05:42,607
<i>Remember how the five of
us used to go hunting every fall</i>

70
00:05:42,642 --> 00:05:45,243
<i>up around Echo Lake?</i>

71
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:46,745
<i>What I'm going to tell you</i>

72
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:48,257
<i>happened on the trip you missed,</i>

73
00:05:48,281 --> 00:05:51,516
<i>because some judge
switched a trial date on you.</i>

74
00:05:51,551 --> 00:05:53,518
<i>Remember that?</i>

75
00:05:55,722 --> 00:05:57,021
Say cheese, boys!

76
00:05:57,056 --> 00:05:58,957
Cheese, boys.

77
00:06:00,494 --> 00:06:02,305
Hey, next time, leave
your shotgun at home, D.B.

78
00:06:02,329 --> 00:06:03,573
The only shooting
you're doing these days

79
00:06:03,597 --> 00:06:05,096
is with that damn camera.

80
00:06:05,131 --> 00:06:06,776
<i>Things would have
turned out so differently</i>

81
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:08,645
<i>if you'd been along
that weekend, Ben.</i>

82
00:06:08,669 --> 00:06:12,103
<i>But it was just me,
Bud, Jimmy and Lucas.</i>

83
00:06:12,138 --> 00:06:14,639
Who's driving? I
am! Are you crazy?

84
00:06:14,675 --> 00:06:17,342
<i>Bud started passing the bourbon,</i>

85
00:06:17,377 --> 00:06:20,345
<i>and nobody much cared that
we hadn't shot a thing that day.</i>

86
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,514
<i>But we didn't exactly go back</i>

87
00:06:22,549 --> 00:06:24,683
<i>to the cabin
empty-handed that night.</i>

88
00:06:24,718 --> 00:06:28,119
<i>On the drive back, we
picked up a hitchhiker.</i>

89
00:06:28,155 --> 00:06:31,389
<i>A girl, very young, very pretty,</i>

90
00:06:31,424 --> 00:06:33,524
<i>very sociable.</i>

91
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:36,094
<i>The guys talked her
into staying a while.</i>

92
00:06:36,129 --> 00:06:38,230
<i>I didn't like the idea,</i>

93
00:06:38,265 --> 00:06:41,599
<i>having just gotten
engaged to Alicia,</i>

94
00:06:41,635 --> 00:06:44,135
<i>but I was outnumbered.</i>

95
00:06:44,171 --> 00:06:47,138
<i>Within a few hours,
everyone was pretty drunk.</i>

96
00:06:47,174 --> 00:06:49,641
<i>I could see where
this was heading.</i>

97
00:06:49,676 --> 00:06:53,411
<i>So could the girl.
She wanted to go.</i>

98
00:06:53,446 --> 00:06:54,746
<i>I got out of there, Ben.</i>

99
00:06:54,781 --> 00:06:59,116
<i>I just got in my car
and drove home.</i>

100
00:06:59,152 --> 00:07:01,219
<i>I didn't even bother
to pack my gear.</i>

101
00:07:01,254 --> 00:07:03,922
<i>I knew something terrible
was going to happen.</i>

102
00:07:05,258 --> 00:07:06,991
<i>I was right.</i>

103
00:07:07,027 --> 00:07:09,994
<i>A few days later, the
enclosed article appeared</i>

104
00:07:10,030 --> 00:07:12,697
<i>in the</i> Atlanta Examiner.

105
00:07:12,733 --> 00:07:15,100
<i>It was her.</i>

106
00:07:15,135 --> 00:07:16,701
<i>They arrested some poor black</i>

107
00:07:16,736 --> 00:07:18,648
<i>who lived a couple
of miles away.</i>

108
00:07:18,672 --> 00:07:19,971
<i>He swore he was innocent,</i>

109
00:07:20,006 --> 00:07:22,340
<i>but he was convicted
and sent to prison.</i>

110
00:07:22,376 --> 00:07:25,710
<i>All these years, Ben, I
lived with the knowledge</i>

111
00:07:25,745 --> 00:07:28,547
<i>that one of our friends,
our closest friends,</i>

112
00:07:28,582 --> 00:07:30,482
<i>could very well be a murderer.</i>

113
00:07:30,517 --> 00:07:34,286
<i>Please, please do what I
never had the guts to do.</i>

114
00:07:34,321 --> 00:07:36,555
<i>Find out the truth.</i>

115
00:07:36,590 --> 00:07:37,788
Mr. Matlock!

116
00:07:37,824 --> 00:07:42,461
Now that the court has
your attention, Mr. Matlock,

117
00:07:42,496 --> 00:07:44,107
would you like to
question Mr. Morgan?

118
00:07:44,131 --> 00:07:47,198
Oh, uh, yes.

119
00:07:47,234 --> 00:07:51,002
I, uh, I apologize for my
lack of attention, Your Honor,

120
00:07:51,037 --> 00:07:54,706
and, uh, I'd be happy
to talk to Mr. Morgan.

121
00:08:01,415 --> 00:08:03,214
Conrad.

122
00:08:03,250 --> 00:08:06,517
What do you think about when
you hear the words, "business card"?

123
00:08:06,553 --> 00:08:07,696
What's up?

124
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:10,188
"Have a problem?"

125
00:08:10,323 --> 00:08:12,324
"Conrad McMasters,

126
00:08:12,359 --> 00:08:13,669
Private Eye." I'm impressed.

127
00:08:13,693 --> 00:08:14,993
Yeah. Pretty good, huh?

128
00:08:15,028 --> 00:08:17,329
Yeah. What's up?

129
00:08:17,364 --> 00:08:19,508
Uh, I want you to
find somebody for me.

130
00:08:19,532 --> 00:08:21,233
Fellow named Calvin Stokes.

131
00:08:21,268 --> 00:08:24,135
He was convicted of
first degree murder in '56.

132
00:08:24,170 --> 00:08:26,082
Spent time at the
Georgia State Pen

133
00:08:26,106 --> 00:08:28,006
and was paroled in '86.

134
00:08:28,041 --> 00:08:29,541
No problem.

135
00:08:29,576 --> 00:08:31,610
Tell him I want to talk to him.

136
00:08:31,645 --> 00:08:33,445
No problem. What about?

137
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:35,413
Close the door.

138
00:08:48,761 --> 00:08:51,262
Well, look. Speaking of
the devil, look who's here.

139
00:08:51,298 --> 00:08:53,031
Pardon the expression.

140
00:08:53,066 --> 00:08:54,510
It's about time.

141
00:08:54,534 --> 00:08:56,745
We were just talking about
your 28th birthday party, Ben.

142
00:08:56,769 --> 00:08:58,035
Remember that?

143
00:08:58,071 --> 00:08:59,737
You missed it completely.

144
00:08:59,773 --> 00:09:01,572
Oh. Nice one.

145
00:09:01,608 --> 00:09:03,352
Such a great party,
nobody realized

146
00:09:03,376 --> 00:09:05,688
you weren't there until it
was over. At least I didn't.

147
00:09:05,712 --> 00:09:08,146
Oh, this is great.

148
00:09:08,181 --> 00:09:10,949
This is great. We finally
got back together again.

149
00:09:12,218 --> 00:09:13,551
Here.

150
00:09:13,586 --> 00:09:15,920
Okay, well, I'm
afraid, uh, I'm afraid

151
00:09:15,956 --> 00:09:17,666
it's not going to be quite...

152
00:09:17,690 --> 00:09:21,159
the celebration we hoped.

153
00:09:21,194 --> 00:09:23,127
Look at this.

154
00:09:25,398 --> 00:09:29,133
That's, um, a
letter D.B. wrote me

155
00:09:29,169 --> 00:09:31,469
just before he died, all
about that hunting trip

156
00:09:31,504 --> 00:09:35,373
you took without me back in '55.

157
00:09:35,408 --> 00:09:37,976
Including the
part about the girl.

158
00:09:38,011 --> 00:09:39,956
The girl? What girl?

159
00:09:39,980 --> 00:09:41,379
That girl.

160
00:09:45,051 --> 00:09:48,253
D.B. thinks one of us killed
some girl that weekend?

161
00:09:48,288 --> 00:09:50,288
That's crazy.

162
00:09:50,323 --> 00:09:53,724
I spent a lot of time with D.B.
during his final weeks, Ben.

163
00:09:53,759 --> 00:09:55,393
He... he wasn't himself.

164
00:09:55,429 --> 00:09:57,195
I-I had him on morphine...

165
00:09:57,230 --> 00:09:59,363
For pain. He had to
have been hallucinating

166
00:09:59,398 --> 00:10:00,731
when he wrote that.

167
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:03,234
Why did he keep this
clipping all these years?

168
00:10:03,269 --> 00:10:05,748
Ben, there was no girl;

169
00:10:05,772 --> 00:10:08,272
in the car, in the
cabin, or anywhere else.

170
00:10:08,308 --> 00:10:11,610
You, uh, you haven't
told anybody about this?

171
00:10:11,645 --> 00:10:14,144
I'm up for reelection next year.

172
00:10:14,181 --> 00:10:16,548
My investigator,
but, uh, no names,

173
00:10:16,583 --> 00:10:18,049
and he's closed-mouthed.

174
00:10:18,085 --> 00:10:19,283
Your investigator?

175
00:10:19,319 --> 00:10:21,719
Well...

176
00:10:21,754 --> 00:10:23,755
I have to go through with this.

177
00:10:23,790 --> 00:10:26,257
Uh, I mean, I just have to.

178
00:10:26,293 --> 00:10:29,660
It was, uh, D.B.'s last
request, his dying wish,

179
00:10:29,695 --> 00:10:31,429
so I have to do it.

180
00:10:31,464 --> 00:10:34,565
But, you know, if nothing
comes of it, then well, we'll...

181
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:36,034
Hey.

182
00:10:36,069 --> 00:10:37,335
That will be that.

183
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:39,370
Yeah, that will be that.

184
00:10:39,406 --> 00:10:41,450
Well, it's a shame.
You know, if you'd have

185
00:10:41,474 --> 00:10:43,386
been there that weekend
with us, like you should have,

186
00:10:43,410 --> 00:10:44,775
you would know.

187
00:10:44,811 --> 00:10:46,789
You wouldn't have
to go through all this.

188
00:10:46,813 --> 00:10:50,782
Yeah. Yeah.

189
00:10:50,817 --> 00:10:54,285
D.B. was sick.

190
00:10:54,321 --> 00:10:55,654
Yeah.

191
00:10:55,689 --> 00:10:58,590
As I recall, that... that
trip was one of our best.

192
00:10:58,625 --> 00:11:00,791
You mean you actually
brought something home?

193
00:11:00,827 --> 00:11:02,226
Of course we did.

194
00:11:02,262 --> 00:11:04,362
Colds.

195
00:11:06,299 --> 00:11:07,779
From sitting on the
cold, hard ground.

196
00:11:09,502 --> 00:11:11,446
Hey, what do you
say we order, huh?

197
00:11:11,470 --> 00:11:12,603
I'm starved.

198
00:11:12,638 --> 00:11:13,749
What else is new, huh?

199
00:11:13,773 --> 00:11:15,406
Yeah. Hey, Rita?

200
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:16,740
All right. It's pretty simple.

201
00:11:16,776 --> 00:11:19,743
Mr. Calvin Stokes
still lives in Echo Lake.

202
00:11:19,779 --> 00:11:22,546
It was all I could do
to get him to talk to me.

203
00:11:22,581 --> 00:11:24,748
He absolutely doesn't
want to get involved.

204
00:11:24,784 --> 00:11:26,684
Told me to shove off.

205
00:11:26,719 --> 00:11:30,288
No, actually, he told me
to get the hell out of there.

206
00:11:30,323 --> 00:11:34,091
Maybe he's guilty after all.

207
00:11:34,127 --> 00:11:35,492
I don't know about that,

208
00:11:35,528 --> 00:11:37,261
but he sure wants to
put the past behind him

209
00:11:37,297 --> 00:11:39,730
and get on with his life.

210
00:11:39,765 --> 00:11:42,333
Why did D.B. have
to dump this on me?

211
00:11:42,368 --> 00:11:45,369
Why couldn't he have just
left me a nice tie or something?

212
00:11:45,404 --> 00:11:47,604
Well, daggone it.

213
00:11:47,640 --> 00:11:49,451
The man won't talk to me.

214
00:11:49,475 --> 00:11:52,476
I can't help him. Maybe
the boys are right.

215
00:11:52,511 --> 00:11:54,756
D.B. was real sick
at the end of his life.

216
00:11:54,780 --> 00:11:57,348
I'm done with this case.

217
00:11:57,383 --> 00:11:59,161
All right, well, if
something else comes up,

218
00:11:59,185 --> 00:12:00,784
you got my card.

219
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:03,154
Yeah, Conrad
McMasters, Private Eye.

220
00:12:04,457 --> 00:12:05,956
See ya, Ben.

221
00:12:53,807 --> 00:12:55,106
Uh-huh. Yeah.

222
00:12:55,142 --> 00:12:58,276
Okay. And, uh,
yeah, we'll be sure...

223
00:12:58,311 --> 00:13:00,271
Ow! Sorry.

224
00:13:01,948 --> 00:13:03,781
Ow!

225
00:13:03,817 --> 00:13:04,882
Ow!

226
00:13:04,918 --> 00:13:06,128
I'm sorry.

227
00:13:06,152 --> 00:13:07,163
How you doing?

228
00:13:07,187 --> 00:13:08,585
I'm fine.

229
00:13:08,621 --> 00:13:10,922
Did your boys get
a look at my car?

230
00:13:10,957 --> 00:13:12,890
Yeah, what was left of it.

231
00:13:12,926 --> 00:13:16,761
Now if anything occurs to
you, anything at all, call me.

232
00:13:16,796 --> 00:13:18,829
I will.

233
00:13:19,933 --> 00:13:21,332
You be careful, Ben.

234
00:13:21,367 --> 00:13:24,735
Yeah, I-I will. Thanks, pal.

235
00:13:26,272 --> 00:13:27,571
Conrad...

236
00:13:27,606 --> 00:13:29,952
Why don't you go to
the hospital? No, I'm fine.

237
00:13:29,976 --> 00:13:32,377
You don't look fine.

238
00:13:32,412 --> 00:13:34,292
Ow! You look hurt.

239
00:13:37,049 --> 00:13:39,384
How could somebody
blow up my car?

240
00:13:39,419 --> 00:13:41,018
Ignition bomb.

241
00:13:41,053 --> 00:13:42,387
You were lucky.

242
00:13:42,422 --> 00:13:44,355
Bob Brooks was right.

243
00:13:44,391 --> 00:13:46,424
Somebody deliberately
tried to kill me.

244
00:13:46,459 --> 00:13:47,992
You bet.

245
00:13:48,027 --> 00:13:50,461
So, do you have any idea
who might have done it?

246
00:13:50,497 --> 00:13:51,629
No.

247
00:13:51,664 --> 00:13:52,764
Think.

248
00:13:52,799 --> 00:13:55,233
Well, I'm thinking.

249
00:13:55,268 --> 00:13:57,569
You think I'm not
thinking? I'm thinking.

250
00:13:57,604 --> 00:13:59,838
Could you, uh, could you trace

251
00:13:59,873 --> 00:14:02,574
where that bomb came from?

252
00:14:02,609 --> 00:14:05,576
Yeah, I'll find out
where it came from.

253
00:14:05,611 --> 00:14:07,779
And I'm going to
do something else.

254
00:14:07,814 --> 00:14:09,391
I'm going to talk
to your friends.

255
00:14:09,415 --> 00:14:10,481
Oh, no.

256
00:14:10,516 --> 00:14:12,150
Well, then you do it.

257
00:14:12,185 --> 00:14:14,263
No, these are my
oldest, dearest friends.

258
00:14:14,287 --> 00:14:16,332
And you're investigating
them for a murder.

259
00:14:16,356 --> 00:14:18,056
Oh...

260
00:14:18,091 --> 00:14:20,591
If someone from the
past, just from the past,

261
00:14:20,626 --> 00:14:23,761
was trying to kill you, they'd
have done it a long time ago.

262
00:14:23,796 --> 00:14:26,364
Whoever planted that
bomb, has got to be connected

263
00:14:26,399 --> 00:14:28,666
with what's going on
now, and you know that.

264
00:14:32,939 --> 00:14:36,440
I have to get up.

265
00:14:40,713 --> 00:14:42,347
Conrad,

266
00:14:42,382 --> 00:14:44,148
you're wrong.

267
00:14:44,183 --> 00:14:46,817
You've got to be wrong.

268
00:14:50,289 --> 00:14:51,789
Forget it.

269
00:14:51,825 --> 00:14:54,592
I wish I could,
Mr. Stokes, but I can't.

270
00:14:54,627 --> 00:14:57,362
A man I've trusted
for 40 years told me

271
00:14:57,397 --> 00:14:59,364
that you went to
prison for a crime

272
00:14:59,399 --> 00:15:01,165
you most probably didn't commit.

273
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:03,367
You're damn right I didn't.

274
00:15:03,402 --> 00:15:05,269
Why won't you tell
me what happened?

275
00:15:05,304 --> 00:15:07,016
Now, what's talking
gonna get me?

276
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:09,018
Now, I did my
time... hard time...

277
00:15:09,042 --> 00:15:11,910
And I just want to forget it.

278
00:15:11,945 --> 00:15:13,244
But if you're innocent...

279
00:15:13,279 --> 00:15:14,946
Keep it down.

280
00:15:14,981 --> 00:15:17,493
Nobody here knows I'm an ex-con.

281
00:15:17,517 --> 00:15:19,484
And I want to keep it that way.

282
00:15:19,519 --> 00:15:21,286
You want to live
your life that way?

283
00:15:21,321 --> 00:15:23,788
Hoping nobody will find out?
Hoping you won't lose your job?

284
00:15:23,823 --> 00:15:25,390
Why won't you let me help you?

285
00:15:25,425 --> 00:15:26,791
Maybe I can clear your name.

286
00:15:26,826 --> 00:15:29,861
Just leave me alone.

287
00:15:29,896 --> 00:15:34,065
No, Mr. Stokes, I can't.

288
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:37,501
I promised my oldest friend.

289
00:15:37,536 --> 00:15:39,904
Now you're going to take me
and show me what happened,

290
00:15:39,939 --> 00:15:42,272
'cause I'm gonna stay
right here till you do.

291
00:15:44,911 --> 00:15:46,688
Now tell me just
exactly what happened.

292
00:15:46,712 --> 00:15:49,180
Well, I was walking home
from working the night shift

293
00:15:49,215 --> 00:15:51,015
at the mill, like I always did.

294
00:15:51,050 --> 00:15:52,216
I didn't have a car.

295
00:15:52,252 --> 00:15:53,795
I didn't have much
of anything. Yeah.

296
00:15:53,819 --> 00:15:55,419
Anyway, I was in a hurry,

297
00:15:55,454 --> 00:15:57,955
so I took a shortcut
right down this path.

298
00:15:57,991 --> 00:15:59,790
Mm-hmm. Mm.

299
00:15:59,825 --> 00:16:00,992
All right.

300
00:16:01,027 --> 00:16:04,428
Right here, right off the path,

301
00:16:04,464 --> 00:16:06,998
I saw a pair of gloves
kind of caught in the bushes

302
00:16:07,033 --> 00:16:08,732
like somebody tossed them there.

303
00:16:08,767 --> 00:16:10,234
So I went and put them on.

304
00:16:10,269 --> 00:16:11,535
Hell, finders keepers.

305
00:16:11,570 --> 00:16:13,282
It was a cold
morning. Of course.

306
00:16:13,306 --> 00:16:16,073
When I got here, I saw
something a little ways down there.

307
00:16:16,109 --> 00:16:17,741
So I went and looked.

308
00:16:17,776 --> 00:16:21,445
And I saw it was a girl
lying facedown in the brush.

309
00:16:21,481 --> 00:16:23,580
I started pulling her out.

310
00:16:23,616 --> 00:16:25,416
Then I realized her
head was all bloody,

311
00:16:25,451 --> 00:16:27,151
and she was stiff
like she was dead.

312
00:16:27,186 --> 00:16:28,852
Now that scared
the hell out of me.

313
00:16:28,888 --> 00:16:30,087
So I left her there,

314
00:16:30,123 --> 00:16:32,623
and I just ran right
up this hill to the road.

315
00:16:32,658 --> 00:16:34,525
Yeah. Uh-huh.

316
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:35,560
Yeah.

317
00:16:37,930 --> 00:16:41,766
And you ran back
to the road here.

318
00:16:41,801 --> 00:16:43,033
Then what?

319
00:16:43,068 --> 00:16:44,179
Well, I kept on running.

320
00:16:44,203 --> 00:16:46,537
Pretty soon I heard a
car coming up behind me.

321
00:16:46,572 --> 00:16:47,971
And it was the sheriff.

322
00:16:48,007 --> 00:16:49,473
Well, then I just panicked.

323
00:16:49,509 --> 00:16:51,108
Started running faster.

324
00:16:51,144 --> 00:16:53,877
Then I looked down and
saw the blood from that girl

325
00:16:53,913 --> 00:16:55,946
was all over my new gloves.

326
00:16:55,981 --> 00:16:58,715
So I just took them off and
threw them in the bushes.

327
00:16:58,751 --> 00:17:01,385
Yeah, and then the
sheriff retrieved them.

328
00:17:01,421 --> 00:17:04,255
And after he ran you
down and arrested you,

329
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:06,657
he turned them
over to the prosecutor

330
00:17:06,692 --> 00:17:09,126
because without them,

331
00:17:09,162 --> 00:17:11,962
they wouldn't have
had much of a case.

332
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:17,079
Do you really think
you can clear my name?

333
00:17:17,103 --> 00:17:20,271
Well, I'll tell you one thing...

334
00:17:20,306 --> 00:17:24,608
I'm going to give it
my very best shot.

335
00:17:29,882 --> 00:17:32,884
Where on earth did you get this?

336
00:17:32,919 --> 00:17:36,153
Ellen Kingsley let me look
through D.B.'s old photo albums.

337
00:17:36,188 --> 00:17:37,788
Remember what a
camera nut he was.

338
00:17:37,824 --> 00:17:39,067
Yeah. Yeah.

339
00:17:39,091 --> 00:17:40,791
How come you're not in this?

340
00:17:40,827 --> 00:17:43,427
Well, that was the weekend I
couldn't make it, remember?

341
00:17:43,462 --> 00:17:45,396
And that-that's,
that's the weekend

342
00:17:45,431 --> 00:17:47,231
D.B. wrote about in that letter.

343
00:17:47,266 --> 00:17:49,700
Ben, for crying out loud.

344
00:17:49,736 --> 00:17:54,405
Bud... it was the...

345
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:59,043
It was the only thing
D.B. ever asked me to do.

346
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:00,477
D.B. was taking morphine

347
00:18:00,513 --> 00:18:01,624
when he wrote that thing.

348
00:18:01,648 --> 00:18:03,113
He was out of his mind.

349
00:18:03,148 --> 00:18:04,493
Excuse me, Bud.

350
00:18:04,517 --> 00:18:06,028
Senator Jacob's
office just called.

351
00:18:06,052 --> 00:18:08,953
Can you make it to lunch with
him and the governor Monday?

352
00:18:08,988 --> 00:18:10,488
Bet I can.

353
00:18:10,523 --> 00:18:11,934
Oh, Senator Jacobs
and the governor...

354
00:18:11,958 --> 00:18:13,491
That's life in the fast lane.

355
00:18:13,526 --> 00:18:16,326
Well, if everything
goes as planned,

356
00:18:16,362 --> 00:18:20,031
in three years, Patty and I will
be moving to Washington DC.

357
00:18:20,066 --> 00:18:21,799
All right!

358
00:18:23,469 --> 00:18:24,868
Oh, wow.

359
00:18:28,975 --> 00:18:34,845
Oh, Bud, I feel
lousy about this,

360
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:37,748
but I-I just got to
go ahead with it. I...

361
00:18:37,783 --> 00:18:42,386
Ben, there was no girl.

362
00:18:42,421 --> 00:18:45,823
Well, just for a moment,

363
00:18:45,858 --> 00:18:52,063
um, let's suppose
there was a girl.

364
00:18:52,098 --> 00:18:54,531
Let's suppose that you
and Lucas and Jimmy

365
00:18:54,567 --> 00:18:56,133
got her up to that cabin,

366
00:18:56,169 --> 00:18:58,469
and there was a lot
of drinking going on.

367
00:18:59,639 --> 00:19:02,572
This is as hard to think about

368
00:19:02,609 --> 00:19:04,742
as it is to say, but suppose...

369
00:19:06,646 --> 00:19:09,080
something happened

370
00:19:09,115 --> 00:19:14,051
that could have
destroyed your reputations?

371
00:19:14,086 --> 00:19:18,055
Suppose you were
afraid the girl would tell?

372
00:19:18,090 --> 00:19:19,568
We've known each
other for 40 years.

373
00:19:19,592 --> 00:19:21,759
How can you suggest
something like that?

374
00:19:21,794 --> 00:19:25,028
I know, but a bunch of young
men getting drunk like that...

375
00:19:25,064 --> 00:19:26,530
Egging one another on.

376
00:19:26,566 --> 00:19:28,132
The next thing you know...

377
00:19:28,167 --> 00:19:29,433
I don't drink.

378
00:19:29,468 --> 00:19:30,968
Templin's Bar.

379
00:19:31,004 --> 00:19:32,769
There was no alcohol in it.

380
00:19:32,805 --> 00:19:35,372
Maybe not now, but
you sure did then.

381
00:19:35,407 --> 00:19:37,542
No, sir. I had stopped by then.

382
00:19:37,577 --> 00:19:39,476
No, no.

383
00:19:39,512 --> 00:19:41,946
Here. Here.

384
00:19:41,981 --> 00:19:44,381
Do you see that?

385
00:19:44,417 --> 00:19:46,551
The flask coming out of
your pocket right there?

386
00:19:46,586 --> 00:19:48,218
You see that?

387
00:19:48,254 --> 00:19:49,954
You always had that. See that?

388
00:19:51,924 --> 00:19:56,761
Now that I'm
remembering that weekend,

389
00:19:56,796 --> 00:19:59,596
it seemed that everybody
changed after that.

390
00:19:59,632 --> 00:20:01,799
You ran for city council.

391
00:20:01,834 --> 00:20:04,068
Lucas went back
to medical school.

392
00:20:04,103 --> 00:20:06,170
Jimmy joined the church.

393
00:20:06,205 --> 00:20:08,839
Stopped chasing the girls.

394
00:20:10,877 --> 00:20:14,645
That's pure coincidence, Ben.

395
00:20:16,683 --> 00:20:20,784
I hope so, Bud.

396
00:20:20,820 --> 00:20:22,687
I really hope so.

397
00:20:26,759 --> 00:20:28,693
What do you mean

398
00:20:28,728 --> 00:20:30,627
I changed for the better
after that weekend?

399
00:20:30,663 --> 00:20:32,074
Well, that's what Bud said.

400
00:20:32,098 --> 00:20:33,497
Buddy said that?

401
00:20:33,532 --> 00:20:36,567
Well, not in so many words,
but you did join the church

402
00:20:36,602 --> 00:20:38,313
right after that weekend,
didn't you, Jimmy?

403
00:20:38,337 --> 00:20:39,503
Well, sure, but...

404
00:20:39,538 --> 00:20:41,016
And you stopped
chasing the girls.

405
00:20:41,040 --> 00:20:42,372
Now wait a minute.

406
00:20:42,408 --> 00:20:45,209
You make it sound like I
was some kind of Don Juan.

407
00:20:45,244 --> 00:20:46,777
Well, you were.

408
00:20:46,813 --> 00:20:48,645
Always looking at the girls,

409
00:20:48,681 --> 00:20:50,592
and always combing
your hair just right

410
00:20:50,616 --> 00:20:52,594
to be sure they
looked back at you.

411
00:20:52,618 --> 00:20:54,496
That's why you never
wore a hat, remember?

412
00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:56,065
Didn't want to
mess up your hair.

413
00:20:56,089 --> 00:20:57,955
Yeah, well, I was just a kid.

414
00:20:57,990 --> 00:21:00,024
Yeah, look at this here.

415
00:21:00,059 --> 00:21:01,992
Huh? See?

416
00:21:02,028 --> 00:21:04,573
That must have been
taken that weekend.

417
00:21:04,597 --> 00:21:05,996
See your hair?

418
00:21:06,032 --> 00:21:08,043
You must have still
been chasing the girls.

419
00:21:08,067 --> 00:21:09,944
Least that's what Bud thought.

420
00:21:09,968 --> 00:21:12,169
He did, did he? Well, sort of.

421
00:21:12,204 --> 00:21:16,573
And it put me to thinking that
maybe something did happen

422
00:21:16,608 --> 00:21:19,677
between you and
that girl that weekend.

423
00:21:19,712 --> 00:21:23,547
There wasn't any girl
that weekend, Ben.

424
00:21:26,852 --> 00:21:29,153
Beautiful church, Jimmy.

425
00:21:29,188 --> 00:21:30,821
Just beautiful.

426
00:21:30,856 --> 00:21:33,135
I hear that they're
talking about

427
00:21:33,159 --> 00:21:35,704
televising your Sunday services.

428
00:21:35,728 --> 00:21:37,060
Well, I've been approached,

429
00:21:37,096 --> 00:21:39,130
but I don't think
it's going to happen.

430
00:21:39,165 --> 00:21:40,731
I'm just a simple preacher.

431
00:21:40,766 --> 00:21:42,399
Aw, don't be so modest.

432
00:21:42,435 --> 00:21:44,913
You could be the
next Billy Graham.

433
00:21:44,937 --> 00:21:46,504
Ben, excuse me.

434
00:21:46,539 --> 00:21:48,572
Bill, would you put one
of those red hymnals

435
00:21:48,608 --> 00:21:50,074
up on the organist's bench?

436
00:21:50,109 --> 00:21:52,910
Miss Cora gets a little
disturbed if she can't find it.

437
00:21:54,380 --> 00:21:59,082
So I can understand why
D.B.'s letter upset you.

438
00:21:59,118 --> 00:22:03,086
I mean, hell... Oh, I mean heck.

439
00:22:03,122 --> 00:22:05,367
Well, heck, it was bad enough

440
00:22:05,391 --> 00:22:08,091
way back when you
had to watch every step

441
00:22:08,126 --> 00:22:10,828
because your father
was a big bank president,

442
00:22:10,863 --> 00:22:14,131
but I think Bud's right.

443
00:22:14,166 --> 00:22:16,967
You have ten times
more to lose now.

444
00:22:17,002 --> 00:22:19,036
Bud said that, did he?

445
00:22:19,071 --> 00:22:21,183
Something to that effect.

446
00:22:21,207 --> 00:22:24,775
Well, Jimmy, I've taken
up enough of your time.

447
00:22:24,810 --> 00:22:27,912
I know you've got a lot to
do to get ready for services.

448
00:22:27,947 --> 00:22:29,947
So, I'll see ya.

449
00:22:29,982 --> 00:22:31,415
I'll see you on Sunday?

450
00:22:33,652 --> 00:22:35,686
Couldn't hurt.

451
00:22:35,721 --> 00:22:37,988
Yeah.

452
00:22:38,023 --> 00:22:39,623
Yeah.

453
00:22:41,994 --> 00:22:46,230
Bill, would you please finish
up this side of the church?

454
00:22:46,265 --> 00:22:48,832
I have to make a phone call.

455
00:22:52,404 --> 00:22:54,616
It's just that I
have a lot to lose.

456
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:55,818
I didn't tell him that.

457
00:22:55,842 --> 00:22:57,920
He said you did.
He was bluffing.

458
00:22:57,944 --> 00:23:00,644
He was just trying to get you
to say something about me.

459
00:23:00,680 --> 00:23:02,713
Neither of you told him
about the girl, did you?

460
00:23:02,748 --> 00:23:03,826
I denied she was ever there.

461
00:23:03,850 --> 00:23:05,026
I did, too.

462
00:23:05,050 --> 00:23:06,450
We have nothing to worry about.

463
00:23:06,486 --> 00:23:07,596
Well...

464
00:23:07,620 --> 00:23:09,620
Who's there?

465
00:23:13,859 --> 00:23:16,894
I didn't want to
believe it, I really didn't.

466
00:23:16,929 --> 00:23:20,998
But you did have
a girl in that cabin.

467
00:23:21,033 --> 00:23:23,178
God knows what you did to her.

468
00:23:23,202 --> 00:23:26,270
You're my oldest friends.

469
00:23:26,305 --> 00:23:30,775
35 years ago, you
did something terrible.

470
00:23:30,810 --> 00:23:34,611
Then you conspired
to keep it a secret.

471
00:23:34,647 --> 00:23:40,284
And when it seemed that your
secret would come out in the dark,

472
00:23:40,319 --> 00:23:44,622
one of you... Lucas,
Bud or Jimmy...

473
00:23:44,657 --> 00:23:48,825
One of you tried to kill me.

474
00:24:06,278 --> 00:24:07,823
Ben, I'm sorry,
I'm due in surgery.

475
00:24:07,847 --> 00:24:09,379
Why don't you drop
this whole thing?

476
00:24:09,414 --> 00:24:11,727
I can't... there was a
girl there that weekend.

477
00:24:11,751 --> 00:24:13,383
You all admitted it.

478
00:24:13,418 --> 00:24:14,918
We didn't.

479
00:24:14,954 --> 00:24:16,487
Lucas, I heard you.

480
00:24:16,522 --> 00:24:17,821
You misunderstood.

481
00:24:17,856 --> 00:24:21,525
So you all spent a quiet
weekend alone in that cabin.

482
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:24,006
That's right. Well, how'd
you come home with colds

483
00:24:24,030 --> 00:24:25,607
from sleeping on the ground?

484
00:24:25,631 --> 00:24:27,597
Oh, we were just joking. No.

485
00:24:27,633 --> 00:24:29,811
Something went on that
weekend, and, by damn,

486
00:24:29,835 --> 00:24:32,736
I'm gonna find
out what. All right,

487
00:24:32,771 --> 00:24:34,849
that's it, I've had it with
your paranoid accusations!

488
00:24:34,873 --> 00:24:36,806
This is harassment,
Ben... I call you my friend,

489
00:24:36,842 --> 00:24:39,376
but that doesn't give you
the right to treat me like this.

490
00:25:03,335 --> 00:25:05,102
Do you mind?

491
00:25:10,776 --> 00:25:13,077
Sylvester!

492
00:25:13,112 --> 00:25:15,346
Sorry about the shirt.

493
00:25:15,381 --> 00:25:17,715
I tell him and I tell him,

494
00:25:17,750 --> 00:25:20,818
but he always ends
up wrecking the shirt.

495
00:25:20,853 --> 00:25:25,422
Hear you're in the
market for some fireworks.

496
00:25:25,457 --> 00:25:26,957
That's what this is about?

497
00:25:26,992 --> 00:25:29,271
Well, we couldn't very
well talk in there, could we?

498
00:25:29,295 --> 00:25:31,529
What are you looking for?

499
00:25:31,564 --> 00:25:33,096
Actually, I'm looking for you.

500
00:25:33,132 --> 00:25:35,866
See, somebody tried to kill my
friend with an emission bomb,

501
00:25:35,902 --> 00:25:37,901
and I think they
bought it from you.

502
00:25:37,937 --> 00:25:38,903
I have no idea...

503
00:25:38,938 --> 00:25:39,981
Who'd you sell bombs to...

504
00:25:40,005 --> 00:25:40,971
I'll be seeing you around.

505
00:25:41,006 --> 00:25:42,773
What?! Are you crazy?!

506
00:25:42,808 --> 00:25:44,475
See, I made a phone call,

507
00:25:44,510 --> 00:25:46,255
and if you touch
me one more time,

508
00:25:46,279 --> 00:25:49,146
there's gonna be more cops
here than you ever seen in your life.

509
00:25:49,181 --> 00:25:51,493
Now, you tell me who it was,

510
00:25:51,517 --> 00:25:53,295
and maybe we can
end this like friends.

511
00:25:53,319 --> 00:25:55,386
It was a phone order, my man.

512
00:25:55,421 --> 00:25:57,065
You don't know who
you sold the bomb to?

513
00:25:57,089 --> 00:25:58,589
That's right.

514
00:25:58,624 --> 00:25:59,723
Who paid you?

515
00:25:59,758 --> 00:26:02,326
Look, I leave the
merchandise on the curb.

516
00:26:02,361 --> 00:26:05,195
Car pulls up, guy
opens the door,

517
00:26:05,230 --> 00:26:08,898
takes the merchandise,
leaves the cash, drives off.

518
00:26:08,934 --> 00:26:11,134
No name, no face.

519
00:26:12,404 --> 00:26:14,104
What'd the car look like?

520
00:26:14,139 --> 00:26:15,506
Brown.

521
00:26:15,541 --> 00:26:16,640
Come on.

522
00:26:16,675 --> 00:26:17,907
Mercedes.

523
00:26:17,943 --> 00:26:19,154
Come on.

524
00:26:19,178 --> 00:26:21,211
560SEL.

525
00:26:21,247 --> 00:26:23,380
License number.

526
00:26:23,415 --> 00:26:25,227
You wrote it down...
The guy could've been

527
00:26:25,251 --> 00:26:26,695
leaving you a sack
of newspapers.

528
00:26:32,124 --> 00:26:34,258
Thank you, Sylvester.

529
00:26:34,293 --> 00:26:35,659
Georgia plate:

530
00:26:35,694 --> 00:26:38,195
U-P-L-7-9-0.

531
00:26:44,836 --> 00:26:46,803
How you doing? Hi.

532
00:26:46,838 --> 00:26:48,138
I'm Conrad McMasters.

533
00:26:48,173 --> 00:26:49,506
I'm working for Ben Matlock.

534
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:51,119
I don't have time for this.

535
00:26:51,143 --> 00:26:53,020
Let me just ask
you... one question,

536
00:26:53,044 --> 00:26:55,078
just one question. No.

537
00:26:55,113 --> 00:26:56,513
How could you do it?

538
00:26:56,548 --> 00:26:58,659
What, you've known
the man for 40 years.

539
00:26:58,683 --> 00:27:00,962
You're supposed to be
one of Ben's best friends.

540
00:27:00,986 --> 00:27:02,397
How could you try to kill him?

541
00:27:02,421 --> 00:27:04,265
I did not such thing.
Don't give me that!

542
00:27:04,289 --> 00:27:05,800
You bought the bomb.
That's impossible!

543
00:27:05,824 --> 00:27:07,636
They identified your
car... There's a witness.

544
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:09,004
I'll tell you what I'm gonna do,

545
00:27:09,028 --> 00:27:10,371
I'm gonna call the police
forensics department,

546
00:27:10,395 --> 00:27:11,361
they're gonna come down here,

547
00:27:11,396 --> 00:27:12,641
they're gonna go
through this car

548
00:27:12,665 --> 00:27:15,499
with a fine-tooth comb.
I didn't buy any bombs.

549
00:27:15,534 --> 00:27:17,434
Wait a minute.

550
00:27:17,469 --> 00:27:19,870
A lot of people
could've done it.

551
00:27:19,905 --> 00:27:21,472
I keep my car locked,

552
00:27:21,507 --> 00:27:24,007
but anybody who knows me...

553
00:27:24,042 --> 00:27:28,212
knows I keep a spare
set of keys right there.

554
00:27:28,247 --> 00:27:30,814
Does Ben?

555
00:27:30,849 --> 00:27:34,217
Ben, what you're saying is
that I stole Lucas Sinclair's car,

556
00:27:34,252 --> 00:27:36,553
then used it to pick up a bomb,

557
00:27:36,588 --> 00:27:39,055
which I then
planted in your car.

558
00:27:39,090 --> 00:27:42,225
If I remembered where
that key box was hidden,

559
00:27:42,260 --> 00:27:43,927
you might have, too.

560
00:27:43,962 --> 00:27:45,562
I don't believe this, Ben.

561
00:27:45,597 --> 00:27:47,531
How long have we been friends?

562
00:27:47,566 --> 00:27:49,199
You couldn't pick up that bomb

563
00:27:49,235 --> 00:27:51,146
with your own car,
could you? Ben,

564
00:27:51,170 --> 00:27:53,537
what in the hell
has gotten into you?

565
00:27:53,572 --> 00:27:55,839
Lucas told me
everything that happened

566
00:27:55,874 --> 00:27:57,675
in that cabin that weekend.

567
00:27:57,710 --> 00:28:00,177
Lucas told you? Told you what?

568
00:28:00,212 --> 00:28:01,879
Everything. Nothing happened.

569
00:28:01,914 --> 00:28:04,248
You're fishing, Ben.

570
00:28:04,283 --> 00:28:06,617
Lucas didn't tell you anything.

571
00:28:06,652 --> 00:28:09,920
He told me about the card game.

572
00:28:11,690 --> 00:28:13,330
All right.

573
00:28:13,359 --> 00:28:15,092
So you talked to Lucas,

574
00:28:15,127 --> 00:28:17,995
and he gave you<i> his</i>
version of that weekend.

575
00:28:18,030 --> 00:28:20,175
But I'm not gonna get hung
for something I didn't do.

576
00:28:20,199 --> 00:28:22,466
You've lied ever since
you read D.B.'s letter.

577
00:28:22,501 --> 00:28:24,379
What, am I supposed
to believe you now?

578
00:28:24,403 --> 00:28:28,438
Then I'll have
to prove it to you.

579
00:28:32,310 --> 00:28:36,312
Lucas tell you we
decided to draw cards?

580
00:28:36,348 --> 00:28:37,714
Yeah.

581
00:28:37,749 --> 00:28:39,694
Person who got the ace spades

582
00:28:39,718 --> 00:28:42,063
would take care of the girl?

583
00:28:42,087 --> 00:28:44,855
Did he tell you who
dealt the cards that night?

584
00:28:44,890 --> 00:28:47,156
You did.

585
00:28:47,192 --> 00:28:49,637
Pulled the ace, the
king and the queen,

586
00:28:49,661 --> 00:28:51,561
shuffled 'em,

587
00:28:51,597 --> 00:28:54,230
and dealt 'em.

588
00:28:54,265 --> 00:28:56,199
Your turn.

589
00:28:58,670 --> 00:29:00,770
Ace of spades.

590
00:29:02,441 --> 00:29:04,774
Do it again.

591
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:17,132
It's the ace of spades.

592
00:29:17,156 --> 00:29:18,522
I know.

593
00:29:18,557 --> 00:29:20,090
I've got the queen.

594
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:23,360
There's the king.

595
00:29:23,395 --> 00:29:25,629
You marked the cards.

596
00:29:25,664 --> 00:29:28,932
Just like I did 35
years ago, Ben.

597
00:29:28,967 --> 00:29:32,369
I didn't want to be the one
to get the ace of spades,

598
00:29:32,404 --> 00:29:35,071
so I made damn sure I didn't.

599
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:42,857
<i>This is a hearing on
defendant's extraordinary writ</i>

600
00:29:42,881 --> 00:29:46,149
in the case of<i> The People
of the State of Georgia</i>

601
00:29:46,184 --> 00:29:47,851
<i>v. Calvin Stokes,</i>

602
00:29:47,886 --> 00:29:50,988
case number 7-6-1-5-3-9.

603
00:29:51,023 --> 00:29:52,389
Appearance as counsel?

604
00:29:52,424 --> 00:29:54,702
Brit Hudson, Deputy
District Attorney

605
00:29:54,726 --> 00:29:57,205
on behalf of Fulton
County, Your Honor.

606
00:29:57,229 --> 00:30:00,063
Benjamin Matlock for the
defendant, Calvin Stokes.

607
00:30:00,099 --> 00:30:01,798
Mr. Stokes is present.

608
00:30:01,833 --> 00:30:03,066
Your petition

609
00:30:03,101 --> 00:30:06,269
presents a very unusual
situation, Mr. Matlock.

610
00:30:06,304 --> 00:30:09,105
You understand that I must
make every presumption

611
00:30:09,140 --> 00:30:12,442
in favor of the trial court's
conviction of your client?

612
00:30:12,477 --> 00:30:13,609
Understood, Your Honor,

613
00:30:13,645 --> 00:30:16,079
but I think a horrible
injustice has occurred.

614
00:30:16,114 --> 00:30:19,649
The evidence we'll hear will
demonstrate that to this court.

615
00:30:19,684 --> 00:30:22,452
Very well... you may
call your first witness.

616
00:30:22,487 --> 00:30:25,822
I was with Buddy Carlson,
Lucas Sinclair and D.B. Kingsley.

617
00:30:25,857 --> 00:30:28,825
As you well know, we
used to go hunting together

618
00:30:28,860 --> 00:30:30,994
up at Echo Lake.

619
00:30:31,029 --> 00:30:32,229
Yeah, j-just...

620
00:30:32,264 --> 00:30:34,231
just good, clean fun.

621
00:30:34,266 --> 00:30:36,277
Well, we thought so then.

622
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:38,969
We were young men,
little more than boys.

623
00:30:39,004 --> 00:30:41,638
Like a lot of young
men, we were...

624
00:30:41,673 --> 00:30:43,440
a little wild and reckless.

625
00:30:43,475 --> 00:30:45,442
Sort of, uh, sowing wild oats.

626
00:30:45,477 --> 00:30:47,122
You know what it was like.

627
00:30:47,146 --> 00:30:48,423
We used to go hunting,

628
00:30:48,447 --> 00:30:52,382
we'd play cards a
little, drink a little,

629
00:30:52,418 --> 00:30:55,986
but... we never did anything
we were really ashamed of.

630
00:30:56,021 --> 00:30:57,787
Until...

631
00:30:57,822 --> 00:30:59,322
that weekend.

632
00:31:02,961 --> 00:31:05,329
Pastor Hubert...

633
00:31:05,364 --> 00:31:07,497
Jimmy...

634
00:31:07,533 --> 00:31:10,166
you have a choice.

635
00:31:10,202 --> 00:31:11,768
You know that.

636
00:31:11,803 --> 00:31:15,004
You can lie and
hope your reputation

637
00:31:15,039 --> 00:31:17,374
as a preacher and a good man

638
00:31:17,409 --> 00:31:19,710
will cause this
court to believe you,

639
00:31:19,745 --> 00:31:21,544
or you can...

640
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:23,547
practice what you preach,

641
00:31:23,582 --> 00:31:25,615
tell the truth,

642
00:31:25,651 --> 00:31:28,385
cleanse your conscience,

643
00:31:28,420 --> 00:31:30,153
correct a wrong

644
00:31:30,189 --> 00:31:33,757
that you've held
secret for 35 years.

645
00:31:33,792 --> 00:31:36,993
Can you make that choice?

646
00:31:38,430 --> 00:31:41,197
I've thought of nothing
else for days, Ben.

647
00:31:41,232 --> 00:31:45,468
I haven't eaten, I
haven't slept, but I...

648
00:31:45,503 --> 00:31:48,272
I have prayed.

649
00:31:48,307 --> 00:31:50,840
Will you tell this court

650
00:31:50,876 --> 00:31:53,677
what happened that weekend

651
00:31:53,712 --> 00:31:58,649
in November 1955?

652
00:31:58,684 --> 00:32:01,718
Everything D.B. said
in that letter is true.

653
00:32:01,754 --> 00:32:04,054
There<i> was</i> a girl.

654
00:32:04,089 --> 00:32:08,025
And after D.B. left,
things got a little wild.

655
00:32:09,594 --> 00:32:12,029
<i>She... threatened to tell on us,</i>

656
00:32:12,064 --> 00:32:14,142
<i>said she was going
straight to the police,</i>

657
00:32:14,166 --> 00:32:15,399
<i>that she was gonna...</i>

658
00:32:15,434 --> 00:32:17,935
<i>see us all in jail, so we...</i>

659
00:32:17,970 --> 00:32:20,704
<i>at first, laughed... We
didn't think she meant it.</i>

660
00:32:20,739 --> 00:32:21,872
<i>But...</i>

661
00:32:21,907 --> 00:32:24,641
<i>then she ran out of the cabin.</i>

662
00:32:24,676 --> 00:32:26,343
<i>We went after her.</i>

663
00:32:26,378 --> 00:32:29,078
<i>We... we realized that
she could ruin our lives</i>

664
00:32:29,113 --> 00:32:30,647
<i>before they even begun,</i>

665
00:32:30,682 --> 00:32:32,349
so we...

666
00:32:32,384 --> 00:32:33,683
caught her,

667
00:32:33,719 --> 00:32:36,252
locked her in the
shed behind the cabin

668
00:32:36,287 --> 00:32:38,054
while we...

669
00:32:38,089 --> 00:32:40,390
decided what to do.

670
00:32:40,425 --> 00:32:42,726
Which was what?

671
00:32:42,761 --> 00:32:45,962
Ben, we were so young
and drunk and scared.

672
00:32:48,033 --> 00:32:51,367
No, no, no, there's no
excuse; what I did was wrong.

673
00:32:51,402 --> 00:32:54,337
What we did was a
sin against that poor girl,

674
00:32:54,372 --> 00:32:57,640
and I... I cannot allow
myself to make excuses.

675
00:32:59,244 --> 00:33:01,745
We decided that...

676
00:33:01,780 --> 00:33:04,192
she would carry
out her threat, that...

677
00:33:04,216 --> 00:33:07,684
we'd go to jail, and we...
our lives would be ruined.

678
00:33:07,719 --> 00:33:10,353
We decided we...
couldn't let her go.

679
00:33:10,389 --> 00:33:16,159
So... we decided one of us
was gonna have to kill her.

680
00:33:16,194 --> 00:33:18,295
One of you?

681
00:33:18,330 --> 00:33:19,474
Mm-hmm.

682
00:33:19,498 --> 00:33:21,365
And how did you decide

683
00:33:21,400 --> 00:33:23,500
which one?

684
00:33:23,535 --> 00:33:26,603
I'm ashamed to
say this, I really am.

685
00:33:26,638 --> 00:33:28,938
<i>We played cards.</i>

686
00:33:28,974 --> 00:33:33,110
We took the queen, the king,
the ace of spades from the deck.

687
00:33:33,145 --> 00:33:35,545
Buddy Carlson
shuffled and dealt them.

688
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:38,948
<i>We agreed that
whoever got the ace...</i>

689
00:33:43,322 --> 00:33:46,323
I can't say it.

690
00:33:46,358 --> 00:33:49,893
Whoever got the ace of spades

691
00:33:49,928 --> 00:33:53,697
would murder that
poor, unfortunate girl.

692
00:33:53,732 --> 00:33:55,965
Is that what you can't say?

693
00:34:00,439 --> 00:34:03,873
You must say it out loud,
so the court can hear, Jimmy.

694
00:34:03,908 --> 00:34:05,075
You must.

695
00:34:08,013 --> 00:34:10,180
Whoever got the ace...

696
00:34:10,215 --> 00:34:12,282
would murder the girl.

697
00:34:16,988 --> 00:34:20,723
Um, then what
happened, Pastor Hubert?

698
00:34:22,460 --> 00:34:23,927
We, uh...

699
00:34:23,962 --> 00:34:27,363
slept in the woods that
night, so that none of us

700
00:34:27,399 --> 00:34:31,734
<i>except the murderer
would know who killed her.</i>

701
00:34:31,770 --> 00:34:33,904
<i>I didn't even look at my card</i>

702
00:34:33,939 --> 00:34:38,041
<i>until I got to the place
where I was gonna sleep.</i>

703
00:34:39,778 --> 00:34:42,378
<i>It was the ace.</i>

704
00:34:43,682 --> 00:34:46,883
<i>So I went to the
shed and unlocked it.</i>

705
00:34:46,919 --> 00:34:51,855
<i>The girl was sitting in
there inside, terrified.</i>

706
00:34:54,226 --> 00:34:57,327
<i>Almost as terrified as I was.</i>

707
00:34:59,264 --> 00:35:04,334
<i>I stood there, frozen.</i>

708
00:35:04,369 --> 00:35:06,736
<i>And I let her go.</i>

709
00:35:06,771 --> 00:35:08,104
What did you say?

710
00:35:08,139 --> 00:35:09,917
She ran past me out the door,

711
00:35:09,941 --> 00:35:12,442
and I didn't stop
her, I let her go.

712
00:35:12,477 --> 00:35:13,777
I couldn't kill her.

713
00:35:13,812 --> 00:35:15,345
Ben, I couldn't kill her.

714
00:35:15,380 --> 00:35:18,582
I was a sinner, but
I was no murderer.

715
00:35:18,617 --> 00:35:21,785
Oh, come on, Jimmy, you
expect us to believe that?

716
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:23,865
It's the truth... I let her go.

717
00:35:23,889 --> 00:35:25,521
And I never saw her again.

718
00:35:25,557 --> 00:35:27,724
Till I saw the paper
several days later,

719
00:35:27,759 --> 00:35:29,092
and read that somebody else

720
00:35:29,127 --> 00:35:30,961
had murdered her.

721
00:35:30,996 --> 00:35:32,262
You mean...

722
00:35:32,297 --> 00:35:34,997
all these years, and
you've never told the others

723
00:35:35,032 --> 00:35:37,032
it wasn't you
who killed the girl?

724
00:35:37,068 --> 00:35:39,013
There was no
reason to tell them.

725
00:35:39,037 --> 00:35:41,237
They wouldn't have
believed me, anyway.

726
00:35:41,272 --> 00:35:44,608
They'd have thought
I just... covered it up.

727
00:35:46,778 --> 00:35:49,813
I h... I have...

728
00:35:49,848 --> 00:35:52,582
no further questions
for this witness.

729
00:35:52,617 --> 00:35:54,317
Witness may step down.

730
00:35:54,352 --> 00:35:57,253
They had the real
murderer all along.

731
00:35:57,289 --> 00:36:00,323
Should've quit while
you were ahead.

732
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:03,827
Three friendships,

733
00:36:03,862 --> 00:36:07,197
three lives destroyed.

734
00:36:07,232 --> 00:36:10,500
For nothing.

735
00:36:12,537 --> 00:36:13,970
Your Honor.

736
00:36:14,006 --> 00:36:17,846
The witness is excused.

737
00:36:23,181 --> 00:36:24,480
You wrecked my life!

738
00:36:24,516 --> 00:36:26,750
Now not only does
everybody know I'm an ex-con,

739
00:36:26,785 --> 00:36:28,852
but they think
I'm guilty as hell!

740
00:36:28,887 --> 00:36:31,065
Well, well, maybe
it's not over yet.

741
00:36:31,089 --> 00:36:32,322
It is for me!

742
00:36:32,357 --> 00:36:35,591
Now I have to find a new
place, a new job and a new home

743
00:36:35,627 --> 00:36:37,060
and start all over again!

744
00:36:37,095 --> 00:36:38,839
Well, yeah, but
if... No! No more!

745
00:36:38,863 --> 00:36:41,664
You know, you know
what I really kick myself for?

746
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:42,932
For being a sucker!

747
00:36:42,967 --> 00:36:44,712
No, you made me
hope for something

748
00:36:44,736 --> 00:36:46,514
that I had no
business hoping for!

749
00:36:46,538 --> 00:36:49,305
So from now on, leave me alone!

750
00:36:59,084 --> 00:37:00,884
Well...

751
00:37:00,919 --> 00:37:03,479
Well, we got to do something.

752
00:37:06,424 --> 00:37:07,556
What is that?

753
00:37:07,592 --> 00:37:09,937
Everything I could get
from the original trial.

754
00:37:09,961 --> 00:37:14,264
Newspaper clippings and
pictures of the, of the evidence

755
00:37:14,299 --> 00:37:16,244
and pictures of the victim.

756
00:37:16,268 --> 00:37:19,735
And-and here are,
here are, uh, pictures

757
00:37:19,771 --> 00:37:21,249
that D.B. took that day.

758
00:37:21,273 --> 00:37:24,307
You lay them out over
there, and I'll lay all these out.

759
00:37:25,610 --> 00:37:29,379
And we'll pray heaven
we'll see something.

760
00:37:29,414 --> 00:37:32,648
Do you see a good
picture of those gloves

761
00:37:32,684 --> 00:37:33,794
that he...

762
00:37:35,654 --> 00:37:37,253
Yeah, yeah.

763
00:37:37,289 --> 00:37:39,623
Yeah, that's
good, that's... yeah.

764
00:37:39,658 --> 00:37:40,790
Yeah.

765
00:37:40,826 --> 00:37:42,292
Now see if you can find

766
00:37:42,327 --> 00:37:45,027
picture was dated
that Sunday morning.

767
00:37:45,062 --> 00:37:46,028
Yeah, there.

768
00:37:46,063 --> 00:37:47,103
That's it, that's it.

769
00:37:47,132 --> 00:37:49,566
Okay.

770
00:37:49,601 --> 00:37:52,235
Yeah.

771
00:37:52,270 --> 00:37:53,236
Uh-huh.

772
00:37:53,271 --> 00:37:54,271
What?

773
00:37:54,306 --> 00:37:55,316
Uh-huh.

774
00:37:55,340 --> 00:37:56,484
What? Let's go.

775
00:37:56,508 --> 00:37:58,108
What do you got?

776
00:37:58,143 --> 00:38:00,621
Well, something good I hope.

777
00:38:00,645 --> 00:38:04,080
We need to see...
the weatherman.

778
00:38:12,491 --> 00:38:15,492
So then you drew
cards to determine

779
00:38:15,527 --> 00:38:17,627
which one of you would kill her.

780
00:38:17,662 --> 00:38:20,163
Yes, I'm sorry to say we did.

781
00:38:20,198 --> 00:38:24,433
B-Before you drew them,
unbeknownst to the others,

782
00:38:24,469 --> 00:38:28,104
to make sure you did
not get the ace of spades,

783
00:38:28,140 --> 00:38:29,205
you marked it.

784
00:38:29,240 --> 00:38:30,873
Is that correct?

785
00:38:30,909 --> 00:38:32,609
I didn't want to kill her.

786
00:38:32,644 --> 00:38:37,947
Well, you were the
nephew of the mayor

787
00:38:37,982 --> 00:38:42,685
from a... highly
respected, influential family

788
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:45,522
with a reputation
to be protected,

789
00:38:45,557 --> 00:38:49,926
not to mention your
own political aspirations.

790
00:38:49,961 --> 00:38:51,873
I did not want
to kill that girl.

791
00:38:51,897 --> 00:38:53,196
I think you did.

792
00:38:53,231 --> 00:38:55,765
What you didn't want was
for the others to know about it.

793
00:38:55,801 --> 00:38:57,078
Objection.

794
00:38:57,102 --> 00:38:58,579
That's not a question,
it's a statement.

795
00:38:58,603 --> 00:38:59,780
Sustained.

796
00:38:59,804 --> 00:39:02,639
I remind you, Mr. Matlock,
that this is not a trial.

797
00:39:02,674 --> 00:39:05,742
Uh, since you knew

798
00:39:05,777 --> 00:39:07,844
which card was the ace,

799
00:39:07,879 --> 00:39:11,448
you knew that Jimmy
Hubert had drawn it,

800
00:39:11,483 --> 00:39:12,449
didn't you?

801
00:39:12,484 --> 00:39:14,117
Yes. And you followed him

802
00:39:14,152 --> 00:39:15,629
to the shed that
night, didn't you?

803
00:39:15,653 --> 00:39:16,619
No.

804
00:39:16,654 --> 00:39:18,188
And when you saw that he was

805
00:39:18,223 --> 00:39:21,457
unable to go through with
it and let the girl run away,

806
00:39:21,493 --> 00:39:22,959
you went after her, didn't you?

807
00:39:22,994 --> 00:39:23,994
No, I did not.

808
00:39:24,028 --> 00:39:25,762
And you caught her
somewhere in the brush,

809
00:39:25,797 --> 00:39:27,197
and you hit her,

810
00:39:27,232 --> 00:39:29,799
and she was dead, and
you left her there, didn't you?

811
00:39:29,834 --> 00:39:31,801
- No, I did not.
- Your Honor,

812
00:39:31,836 --> 00:39:33,581
where is defense
counsel going with all this?

813
00:39:33,605 --> 00:39:36,217
That is the court's question
as well, Mr. Matlock.

814
00:39:36,241 --> 00:39:37,885
You're not meeting
your burden of proof

815
00:39:37,909 --> 00:39:39,242
by these off-the-wall inquiries.

816
00:39:39,277 --> 00:39:41,455
Do you have something
more concrete?

817
00:39:41,479 --> 00:39:43,324
Yes, I do, Your
Honor. Then get to it,

818
00:39:43,348 --> 00:39:44,547
would you please?

819
00:39:44,582 --> 00:39:46,649
Yes, Your Honor.

820
00:39:46,684 --> 00:39:50,854
I believe you all have a
copy of these pictures.

821
00:39:50,889 --> 00:39:52,856
Uh, the gloves

822
00:39:52,891 --> 00:39:53,968
that you were
wearing that weekend,

823
00:39:53,992 --> 00:39:54,958
what'd they look like?

824
00:39:54,993 --> 00:39:56,292
That was 35 years ago.

825
00:39:56,328 --> 00:39:57,293
Who remembers?

826
00:39:57,328 --> 00:40:00,696
Well, let me ask you this, then.

827
00:40:00,731 --> 00:40:02,965
This is a picture of, uh,

828
00:40:03,001 --> 00:40:06,468
Jimmy Hubert, Lucas
Sinclair and you,

829
00:40:06,504 --> 00:40:07,804
with your gloves on.

830
00:40:07,839 --> 00:40:09,683
Now, th-this picture was
made that weekend, wasn't it?

831
00:40:09,707 --> 00:40:10,718
Yes. D.B. took it

832
00:40:10,742 --> 00:40:12,319
and sent copies
to all three of us.

833
00:40:12,343 --> 00:40:13,309
Right,

834
00:40:13,344 --> 00:40:14,610
right, right.

835
00:40:14,645 --> 00:40:18,781
You know, when you
compare your gloves...

836
00:40:18,817 --> 00:40:21,250
There's a blowup of
one of your gloves...

837
00:40:21,286 --> 00:40:24,187
With the gloves that,
uh, Calvin Stokes had

838
00:40:24,222 --> 00:40:26,655
when the sheriff arrested
him at the scene of the crime,

839
00:40:26,691 --> 00:40:30,392
you find something
very interesting.

840
00:40:30,428 --> 00:40:32,095
They're the same gloves.

841
00:40:32,130 --> 00:40:34,430
They're the same brand, maybe.

842
00:40:34,466 --> 00:40:35,432
No.

843
00:40:35,467 --> 00:40:36,733
The same gloves.

844
00:40:36,768 --> 00:40:38,167
The glove in this picture

845
00:40:38,202 --> 00:40:40,247
is the same as the
glove in this picture.

846
00:40:40,271 --> 00:40:41,382
You see that little tear

847
00:40:41,406 --> 00:40:44,006
right along the seam?

848
00:40:44,041 --> 00:40:46,108
Identical.

849
00:40:46,143 --> 00:40:47,488
Then I must have lost it,

850
00:40:47,512 --> 00:40:48,656
and he must have found them.

851
00:40:48,680 --> 00:40:49,912
Oh, you lost them.

852
00:40:49,948 --> 00:40:51,559
Hell, I don't remember;
it's 35 years ago.

853
00:40:51,583 --> 00:40:52,949
I mean, how else would he have

854
00:40:52,984 --> 00:40:53,950
wound up with them?

855
00:40:53,985 --> 00:40:55,117
I tell you how.

856
00:40:55,153 --> 00:40:57,287
'Cause you were
wearing those gloves

857
00:40:57,322 --> 00:41:00,189
when you killed that
girl, got her blood on them

858
00:41:00,224 --> 00:41:01,391
and threw them away.

859
00:41:01,426 --> 00:41:04,026
The next morning, Calvin
Stokes comes along,

860
00:41:04,061 --> 00:41:06,529
he picks them up, and that's
how he wound up with them.

861
00:41:06,564 --> 00:41:07,530
No.

862
00:41:07,565 --> 00:41:08,931
If he found them, it's because

863
00:41:08,966 --> 00:41:11,045
I dropped them in the woods
someplace earlier that day.

864
00:41:11,069 --> 00:41:12,202
No.

865
00:41:12,237 --> 00:41:13,769
You were wearing those gloves

866
00:41:13,805 --> 00:41:15,849
when you left that
shed to kill that girl,

867
00:41:15,873 --> 00:41:17,040
and I can prove it.

868
00:41:17,075 --> 00:41:18,652
How in the hell
do you know that?

869
00:41:18,676 --> 00:41:23,146
D.B., D.B. took pictures

870
00:41:23,181 --> 00:41:25,381
of every hunt, all
the way through.

871
00:41:25,416 --> 00:41:27,816
He had a camera always
present, remember?

872
00:41:27,852 --> 00:41:29,084
From beginning to end.

873
00:41:29,120 --> 00:41:31,921
Here at the beginning,
here at the end.

874
00:41:31,956 --> 00:41:34,957
Even D.B. got in this
picture using a trip wire.

875
00:41:34,992 --> 00:41:36,092
See the bulb?

876
00:41:36,127 --> 00:41:37,493
See there?

877
00:41:37,528 --> 00:41:38,639
You're wearing your gloves.

878
00:41:38,663 --> 00:41:39,963
You know,

879
00:41:39,998 --> 00:41:42,243
I'm not sure which trip that
picture was taken on, Ben.

880
00:41:42,267 --> 00:41:44,934
It, uh, maybe was
taken on a different trip.

881
00:41:44,969 --> 00:41:46,869
Eh, come on, Bud.

882
00:41:46,905 --> 00:41:48,871
Come on, now.

883
00:41:48,907 --> 00:41:50,907
You know how organized D.B. was.

884
00:41:50,942 --> 00:41:52,909
Here's the original to this one.

885
00:41:52,944 --> 00:41:55,077
He wrote the date on
the back of every picture.

886
00:41:55,112 --> 00:41:56,346
See? 11/55.

887
00:41:56,381 --> 00:41:57,413
November '55.

888
00:41:57,449 --> 00:41:58,948
It's his handwriting, too.

889
00:41:58,983 --> 00:42:01,329
Yes, but, uh, how do you know
it wasn't taken in the morning

890
00:42:01,353 --> 00:42:02,318
when we were starting out?

891
00:42:02,353 --> 00:42:06,055
Because...

892
00:42:06,090 --> 00:42:08,010
what are you
holding in your hand?

893
00:42:09,093 --> 00:42:10,360
It's a flask?

894
00:42:10,395 --> 00:42:12,362
What was in the flask?

895
00:42:12,397 --> 00:42:14,264
Probably bourbon.

896
00:42:14,299 --> 00:42:17,032
You know, there was
one hard and fast rule

897
00:42:17,068 --> 00:42:20,169
we had about
alcohol and hunting.

898
00:42:20,204 --> 00:42:22,004
I remember.

899
00:42:22,039 --> 00:42:25,007
No drinking till
the end of the day

900
00:42:25,042 --> 00:42:26,820
when we were all done. Why?

901
00:42:26,844 --> 00:42:30,078
Well, because alcohol
and loaded guns don't mix.

902
00:42:30,114 --> 00:42:31,080
Right.

903
00:42:31,115 --> 00:42:33,348
And you were drinking.

904
00:42:33,384 --> 00:42:34,350
See?

905
00:42:34,385 --> 00:42:37,120
Cap is off the flask.

906
00:42:37,155 --> 00:42:39,288
Since you were drinking,

907
00:42:39,324 --> 00:42:41,858
it must have been the
end of the day, right?

908
00:42:41,893 --> 00:42:44,026
I don't remember.

909
00:42:44,062 --> 00:42:45,929
Well, you got your gloves on.

910
00:42:45,964 --> 00:42:50,366
And you got your gloves on here.

911
00:42:50,402 --> 00:42:53,502
And the date on that
picture is the same.

912
00:42:53,538 --> 00:42:56,472
11/55.

913
00:42:56,507 --> 00:42:59,575
All the fellas are wearing
the same stuff they're wearing

914
00:42:59,610 --> 00:43:02,711
in all the other pictures.

915
00:43:02,747 --> 00:43:03,880
Must have been cold

916
00:43:03,915 --> 00:43:06,649
in that cabin that night.

917
00:43:06,684 --> 00:43:08,751
You got your gloves on.

918
00:43:08,786 --> 00:43:11,098
Well, that's because the picture
was taken the night before.

919
00:43:11,122 --> 00:43:13,189
It was on Friday
when we first arrived.

920
00:43:13,224 --> 00:43:14,224
No.

921
00:43:14,258 --> 00:43:15,703
The picture was
taken on Saturday

922
00:43:15,727 --> 00:43:17,805
after you locked that
poor girl in the shed

923
00:43:17,829 --> 00:43:19,963
and before Jimmy
Hubert went up to kill her.

924
00:43:19,998 --> 00:43:21,575
How in the hell
do you know that?

925
00:43:21,599 --> 00:43:23,700
Because D.B. is
not in the picture.

926
00:43:23,735 --> 00:43:25,168
Well, he was the
one taking it! No!

927
00:43:25,203 --> 00:43:26,680
You were taking
it with the trip wire.

928
00:43:26,704 --> 00:43:27,815
See the bulb in your hand?

929
00:43:27,839 --> 00:43:29,338
See the glove
there with the bulb?

930
00:43:31,442 --> 00:43:34,043
D.B. had long gone.

931
00:43:34,078 --> 00:43:37,479
You probably wanted him
to think that nothing unusual

932
00:43:37,515 --> 00:43:40,349
had happened since he had left.

933
00:43:40,384 --> 00:43:41,684
That's why you took it.

934
00:43:41,719 --> 00:43:44,988
The same thing with this
picture Sunday morning.

935
00:43:45,023 --> 00:43:47,857
Make D.B. think like you
were happy as anything.

936
00:43:47,892 --> 00:43:49,625
See there?

937
00:43:49,661 --> 00:43:51,861
Now, look.

938
00:43:51,896 --> 00:43:56,165
Gloves... gloves...

939
00:43:56,200 --> 00:43:59,435
gloves...

940
00:43:59,471 --> 00:44:01,003
no gloves.

941
00:44:03,074 --> 00:44:05,341
What happened to them?

942
00:44:05,376 --> 00:44:07,510
That picture was taken
Saturday not Sunday,

943
00:44:07,545 --> 00:44:09,856
and I-I just hadn't gotten
around to putting them on yet.

944
00:44:09,880 --> 00:44:11,047
No, it was Sunday.

945
00:44:11,082 --> 00:44:13,682
And again, D.B.
didn't take that picture.

946
00:44:13,717 --> 00:44:14,784
You did.

947
00:44:14,819 --> 00:44:15,785
With the trip wire.

948
00:44:15,820 --> 00:44:17,520
See the bulb in your hand?

949
00:44:17,555 --> 00:44:19,322
And you know something else?

950
00:44:19,357 --> 00:44:22,692
I called the weather
bureau, and that weekend,

951
00:44:22,727 --> 00:44:25,194
that Saturday,

952
00:44:25,229 --> 00:44:29,131
was cool and overcast all day.

953
00:44:29,167 --> 00:44:33,169
But Sunday morning
was bright, sunshiny,

954
00:44:33,204 --> 00:44:34,603
not a cloud in the sky.

955
00:44:34,638 --> 00:44:35,638
You see?

956
00:44:37,675 --> 00:44:39,042
And it was cold that morning.

957
00:44:39,077 --> 00:44:42,779
Sunday morning was
much colder than it was

958
00:44:42,814 --> 00:44:45,481
all day Saturday.

959
00:44:45,516 --> 00:44:49,452
And that... yet there you stand

960
00:44:49,487 --> 00:44:50,920
bare-handed.

961
00:44:50,955 --> 00:44:52,488
Why, Bud?

962
00:44:52,523 --> 00:44:54,457
Why?

963
00:44:58,529 --> 00:45:00,896
I think I know.

964
00:45:00,931 --> 00:45:04,600
You had tossed
your gloves away...

965
00:45:04,635 --> 00:45:06,569
after you murdered

966
00:45:06,604 --> 00:45:09,905
that young
hitchhiker, hadn't you?

967
00:45:16,114 --> 00:45:18,047
Ben, I...

968
00:45:23,788 --> 00:45:27,522
It's hard...

969
00:45:27,558 --> 00:45:31,827
to keep coming up with answers

970
00:45:31,863 --> 00:45:35,264
to these very difficult
questions, isn't it, Bud?

971
00:45:37,035 --> 00:45:40,569
Maybe... maybe you'd better

972
00:45:40,604 --> 00:45:45,208
talk to your lawyer before
you answer that last one.

973
00:45:47,611 --> 00:45:49,545
Yes.

974
00:46:02,427 --> 00:46:03,392
Good luck, Mr. Stokes.

975
00:46:03,428 --> 00:46:04,393
Thank you,

976
00:46:04,428 --> 00:46:05,995
Mr. Matlock.

977
00:46:06,030 --> 00:46:07,229
Conrad.

978
00:46:07,265 --> 00:46:08,830
Take care.

979
00:46:08,866 --> 00:46:10,032
Mr. Matlock.

980
00:46:11,202 --> 00:46:12,834
Well... Yeah.

981
00:46:28,619 --> 00:46:31,120
You know...

982
00:46:31,155 --> 00:46:34,623
you think you know
people, and you don't.

983
00:46:34,658 --> 00:46:38,060
I thought I knew
all those fellas.

984
00:46:38,096 --> 00:46:40,796
All the 25 years
Mr. Stokes was in prison,

985
00:46:40,831 --> 00:46:44,967
I thought I knew them
and that we were friends.

986
00:46:45,002 --> 00:46:46,736
And we weren't.

987
00:46:46,771 --> 00:46:49,739
Funny, huh?

988
00:46:49,774 --> 00:46:51,207
In a way, I guess.

989
00:46:51,242 --> 00:46:52,319
Well,

990
00:46:52,343 --> 00:46:55,244
all you can do is
the best you can.

991
00:46:55,279 --> 00:46:57,646
That's what Mama always told me.

992
00:46:57,681 --> 00:46:58,647
Yeah? Best you can?

993
00:46:58,682 --> 00:46:59,682
You bet.

994
00:47:01,519 --> 00:47:03,552
Hey.

995
00:47:03,587 --> 00:47:05,099
Let's go down to Ray Templin's

996
00:47:05,123 --> 00:47:07,623
and hear a little music.

997
00:47:07,659 --> 00:47:08,659
What say?

998
00:47:08,693 --> 00:47:09,725
Yeah.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
